  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z in [-2, 2]. It is steeper along x, so a learning rate above 2/3 overshoots there and keeps growing. At 1 the run diverges within a few steps.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly 0 at b = 64. The noise is fixed by the seed, the run number and the step.",
    "The orange arrow is the step the ball takes next. The grey arrow is the full-data step, −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at their true length in the bowl's units (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes above 6, the loss at the bowl's corner edge (diverged). The trail shows the start point plus up to 40 landing points.",
    "Points beyond the bowl are drawn at its edge. The loss readout keeps the true value. Five steps run per second while playing. Reset replays the same noise from run 0."
  ]
}